[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":155},"en/routes/historia.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8},"cubos",[9,10,11,12,13,14,15],{"_16":102,"_18":103},{"_16":73,"_18":74},{"_16":63,"_18":64},{"_16":54,"_18":55},{"_16":44,"_18":45},{"_16":35,"_18":36},{"_16":17,"_18":19},"cubo",{"_21":22,"_32":24,"_33":34},"historias",[20],{"_21":22,"_23":24,"_25":26,"_27":28},"id","skewb","titulo","Skewb","html","<p>The Skewb was invented by Tony Durham and released by Uwe Mèffert around 1982, first as the &quot;Pyraminx Cube&quot;; the name Skewb came from Douglas Hofstadter, in his column about the Rubik&#39;s Cube. Unlike cubes, it turns around its corners: each turn moves half of the puzzle. It became an official <a href=\"/en/glossary#wca\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The association that runs the official competitions and keeps the records.\">WCA</a> event in 2014. Sarah Strong&#39;s method, with the <a href=\"/en/glossary#sledgehammer\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Two triggers: the sledgehammer is R' F R F' and the hedgeslammer, F R' F' R. On the Skewb, they make up almost every algorithm in Sarah's…\">sledgehammer</a> and the hedgeslammer, is almost everyone&#39;s path: Intermediate has 10 cases, and Advanced solves the last two layers in one go.</p>\n","fontes",[29,30,31],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skewb","https://www.worldcubeassociation.org/posts/skewb-new-event-in-2014","https://sarah.cubing.net/skewb/my-method","nome","endereco","/skewb/",{"_21":38,"_32":39,"_33":43},[37],{"_21":38,"_23":39,"_25":40,"_27":41},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","<p>The tetrahedron was created and patented by Uwe Mèffert and released by the Japanese company Tomy in 1981, at the height of the Rubik&#39;s Cube craze. Speedsolving methods start with the tips and then build a &quot;V&quot; (two edges and the centers) or a whole layer, as in <a href=\"/en/glossary#top-first\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A layer-by-layer Pyraminx method: the tips and centers, the 1st layer and the 3 edges left (L3E). To go faster, L4E.\">Top-first</a>: hence <a href=\"/en/glossary#l4e\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the Pyraminx: solves the 4 edges left after building the &quot;V&quot; on the bottom (the centers and 2 first-layer edges).\">L4E</a> (the last 4 edges) and <a href=\"/en/glossary#l3e\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the Pyraminx: with the first layer done, solves the 3 remaining edges.\">L3E</a> (the last 3).</p>\n",[42],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyraminx","/pyraminx/",{"_21":47,"_32":48,"_33":53},[46],{"_21":47,"_23":48,"_25":49,"_27":50},"megaminx","Megaminx","<p>The puzzle dodecahedron was invented by several people independently; Uwe Mèffert bought the rights to some of the patents and sells it under the name Megaminx. It has been an official <a href=\"/en/glossary#wca\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The association that runs the official competitions and keeps the records.\">WCA</a> event since 2003. It is solved like a longer 3x3: a &quot;<a href=\"/en/glossary#estrela\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the Megaminx, the 5 edges of one face in place, like the 3x3 cross. It is the first step (the white star) and, in the last layer, the…\">star</a>&quot; of edges (like the <a href=\"/en/glossary#cruz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 4 edges of one face (usually white) in place, lined up with the centers. The first step of the beginner method and of CFOP.\">cross</a>), the layers around it and the last face with algorithms, in 4 steps (<a href=\"/en/glossary#eo\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Edge orientation: each edge turned so that it can go to its place without using F and B. In the last layer (in 2-Look and on the Megaminx),…\">EO</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#co\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Corner orientation: twisting the top corners until the top color is all on the top face.\">CO</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#ep\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Edge permutation: putting the top edges, already oriented, in their right places.\">EP</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#cp\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Corner permutation: putting the top corners, already oriented, in their right places.\">CP</a>, as in the tutorial by Feliks Zemdegs and Andy Klise) or in two (<a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> and <a href=\"/en/glossary#pll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Puts the last layer pieces in place, after OLL. There are 21 cases, named after letters (T, J, Y…); in 2-Look PLL, 6 cases in two parts.\">PLL</a>).</p>\n",[51,52],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaminx","https://www.cubeskills.com/uploads/pdf/tutorials/intermediate-megaminx-techniques.pdf","/megaminx/",{"_21":57,"_32":57,"_33":62},[56],{"_21":57,"_23":58,"_25":59,"_27":60},"5x5","The 5x5 and reduction","<p>The 5x5 (Professor&#39;s Cube) was created by Udo Krell in 1981 and made by Uwe Mèffert from 1983. It is also solved by <a href=\"/en/glossary#reducao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"For 4x4 and 5x5: build the centers and pair up the edges until the cube is like a 3x3, which is solved the usual way.\">reduction</a>: centers, edges (the 3 pieces of each one) and then like a 3x3. Unlike the 4x4, once reduced it has no <a href=\"/en/glossary#paridade\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 4x4 (and even cubes), situations that don't exist on the 3x3: a single flipped edge (OLL Parity) or two swapped pieces (PLL Parity).…\">parity</a>: the hard cases come when pairing the last edges (<a href=\"/en/glossary#l2e\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In the 5x5 reduction: joins the pieces of the last 2 edges, finishing the reduction.\">L2E</a>).</p>\n",[61],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professor%27s_Cube","/5x5/",{"_21":66,"_32":66,"_33":72},[65],{"_21":66,"_23":67,"_25":68,"_27":69},"4x4","The 4x4 and reduction","<p>The 4x4 (Rubik&#39;s Revenge) was created by Péter Sebestény and released in 1981. Since it has no fixed centers, the most common method is <a href=\"/en/glossary#reducao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"For 4x4 and 5x5: build the centers and pair up the edges until the cube is like a 3x3, which is solved the usual way.\">reduction</a>: build the centers, <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a> up the edges and finish like a 3x3. But the 4x4 allows swaps that don&#39;t exist on the 3x3, hence the parities (<a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> <a href=\"/en/glossary#paridade\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 4x4 (and even cubes), situations that don't exist on the 3x3: a single flipped edge (OLL Parity) or two swapped pieces (PLL Parity).…\">Parity</a> and <a href=\"/en/glossary#pll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Puts the last layer pieces in place, after OLL. There are 21 cases, named after letters (T, J, Y…); in 2-Look PLL, 6 cases in two parts.\">PLL</a> Parity). The <a href=\"/en/glossary#yau\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A variation of reduction on the 4x4: two opposite centers, the cross, the other centers and the edges, in that order.\">Yau</a> method, by Robert Yau (2009), is a reduction in a different order (centers and <a href=\"/en/glossary#cruz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 4 edges of one face (usually white) in place, lined up with the centers. The first step of the beginner method and of CFOP.\">cross</a> before the edges).</p>\n",[70,71],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubik%27s_Revenge","https://www.cubeskills.com/uploads/pdf/tutorials/intermediate-tips-and-yau-method.pdf","/4x4/",{"_21":97,"_32":97,"_33":101},[75,76,77,78],{"_21":97,"_23":98,"_25":99,"_27":100},{"_21":91,"_23":92,"_25":93,"_27":94},{"_21":85,"_23":86,"_25":87,"_27":88},{"_21":79,"_23":80,"_25":81,"_27":82},"ortega","Ortega","<p>The method is named after Victor <a href=\"/en/glossary#ortega\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A 2x2 method: it builds one face, orients the top one (Ortega OLL) and permutes both layers at once (PBL).\">Ortega</a>, who spread it (in 2001 it went up on Josef Jelinek&#39;s site), but the idea is older: it comes from a corners-first 3x3 method, published by Jeffrey <a href=\"/en/glossary#varasano\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Another name for Ortega, after Jeffrey Varasano, who published in 1981 the 3x3 corners-first method it comes from.\">Varasano</a> in 1981, which is why it is also called the Varasano method. It is the next step for those who solve the 2x2 by layers: face, <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> and <a href=\"/en/glossary#pbl\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 2x2 (Ortega): puts the pieces of both layers in place at once.\">PBL</a>, with about 12 algorithms.</p>\n",[83,84],"https://speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Ortega_Method","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket_Cube","guimond","Guimond","<p>The method is by Gaétan <a href=\"/en/glossary#guimond\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A 2x2 method: it twists the corners so white and yellow are on top and bottom (mixed), separates the two colors and finishes with Ortega's…\">Guimond</a>, a cuber from Montreal, Canada, who says on his website that he created it in 1982. The idea is the one behind corners-first methods: twist the corners, separate the two colors and only then put each one in place. It caught on because it uses few turns (about 16) and because separation can be predicted during <a href=\"/en/glossary#inspecao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 15 seconds to look at the cube before you start solving, without turning the faces.\">inspection</a>. The last step is the same <a href=\"/en/glossary#pbl\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"On the 2x2 (Ortega): puts the pieces of both layers in place at once.\">PBL</a> as <a href=\"/en/glossary#ortega\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A 2x2 method: it builds one face, orients the top one (Ortega OLL) and permutes both layers at once (PBL).\">Ortega</a>; in 2004, Guimond also proposed a version for the 3x3.</p>\n",[89,90],"https://www.cubinghistory.com/2x2/Methods/Guimond","https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Guimond_Method","cll-eg","CLL and EG","<p><a href=\"/en/glossary#cll-eg\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Fast 2x2 methods. In CLL, with the first layer done, the top one takes a single algorithm. In EG only the first face is needed (the bottom…\">CLL</a> (solving the top layer at once with the bottom one done) had been discussed since 2003; the first site with algorithms made for the 2x2 was Erik Akkersdijk&#39;s, in 2006. EG goes further: with only the bottom face done, it solves everything at once. Łukasz Ciałoń was already solving this way in 2005; Gunnar Krig proposed the idea in 2006 and Akkersdijk published the EG-1 algorithms; the name comes from Erik and Gunnar. The EG-2 ones were generated by David Woner in 2008. It is the method of the fastest 2x2 solvers.</p>\n",[95,96],"https://cubinghistory.com/2x2/Methods/EG","https://cubinghistory.com/2x2/Methods/CLL","2x2","The 2x2","<p>The 2x2 came before the Rubik&#39;s Cube: American Larry Nichols invented a 2×2×2 held together by magnets in 1970, patented in 1972. Years later, the company that owned the patent sued the maker of the Rubik&#39;s Cube, and the court (1986) ruled in its favor in the case of the 2x2.</p>\n",[84],"/2x2/",{"_21":147,"_32":147,"_33":154},[104,105,106,107,108,109,110],{"_21":147,"_23":148,"_25":149,"_27":150},{"_21":141,"_23":142,"_25":143,"_27":144},{"_21":134,"_23":135,"_25":136,"_27":137},{"_21":129,"_23":130,"_25":131,"_27":132},{"_21":123,"_23":124,"_25":125,"_27":126},{"_21":117,"_23":118,"_25":119,"_27":120},{"_21":111,"_23":112,"_25":113,"_27":114},"zb","ZB and ZBLL","<p>In 1981, Lars Petrus proposed orienting the edges first, so the last layer could be done in a single step. Around 2000–2001, Bernard Helmstetter generated the algorithms for these cases, and Zbigniew Zborowski and Ron van Bruchem brought the idea to <a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a> (the ZB method: <a href=\"/en/glossary#zbls\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Inserts the last F2L pair and orients the top edges at the same time, leaving the yellow cross ready for ZBLL.\">ZBLS</a> orients the edges on the last <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a>; <a href=\"/en/glossary#zbll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Solves the whole last layer with one algorithm, when the top edges are already oriented. There are hundreds of cases.\">ZBLL</a> solves the last layer). The name ZBLL appeared in 2005. With 472 cases, for a long time it was something for few; today several of the fastest in the world use it.</p>\n",[115,116],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Steps/ZBLL","https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Steps/ZBLS","ultimo-slot","Last slot: WV, SV and VLS","<p>Ideas for orienting the last layer while inserting the last <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a>, skipping <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a>. The Winter Variation was proposed by Lucas Winter in January 2005; the Summer Variation, by Chester Lian in 2009. Mats Valk had used a larger set since 2009, for the pair ready in any shape, without publishing it; in 2013, Rowe Hessler came to the same idea, and the two split it: <a href=\"/en/glossary#vls\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Like WV, but also with flipped top edges: inserts the last pair and orients the whole last layer.\">VLS</a> (Valk Last <a href=\"/en/glossary#slot\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"One of the 4 spaces in the first two layers where an F2L pair goes.\">Slot</a>) for the pair already built and HLS for the pair one R U R&#39; away from ready. Valk used a VLS algorithm in his 4.74 s world record, in 2016.</p>\n",[121,122],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Steps/OLS","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mats_Valk","roux","Roux","<p>Created by Frenchman Gilles <a href=\"/en/glossary#roux\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Gilles Roux's method: two 1x2x3 blocks on the sides, CMLL and the last 6 edges (LSE) with the middle layer. It uses few moves.\">Roux</a>, who got back into cubing in 2001 and wanted an almost fully intuitive method, finished with just M and U. The idea: build two 1×2×3 <a href=\"/en/glossary#bloco\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A set of pieces solved together; in Roux, the 1x2x3 blocks on the sides.\">blocks</a> on the sides (leaving the middle slice free), solve the top corners (<a href=\"/en/glossary#cmll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In Roux: orients and permutes the 4 top corners, without caring about the top edges or the middle layer.\">CMLL</a>) and finally the last 6 edges (<a href=\"/en/glossary#lse\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In Roux: the last 6 edges (4 on top and 2 in the middle layer), solved with just U and M.\">LSE</a>). He presented the method to the community in August 2003; the name CMLL is his (2005). It is known for using few moves and almost no <a href=\"/en/glossary#rotacao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Turning the whole cube (x, y, z), without moving the pieces relative to each other.\">rotations</a>. Roux was also on the <a href=\"/en/glossary#wca\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The association that runs the official competitions and keeps the records.\">WCA</a> board (2004–2008).</p>\n",[127,128],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Methods/Roux","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilles_Roux","ollcp","OLLCP","<p>Doing <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> while also putting the corners in place was proposed by Masayuki Akimoto and others in the early 2000s (first known mention in 2005). Kenneth Gustavsson published the full list in 2006 under the name XCLL; the community started calling it <a href=\"/en/glossary#ollcp\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Does OLL and puts the corners in place at the same time, leaving only an EPLL.\">OLLCP</a> around 2010.</p>\n",[133],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Steps/OLLCP","cfop","CFOP (Fridrich)","<p><a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a> = <a href=\"/en/glossary#cruz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 4 edges of one face (usually white) in place, lined up with the centers. The first step of the beginner method and of CFOP.\">Cross</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#f2l\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The first two layers. In CFOP, each bottom corner goes together with the middle edge above it (a pair), one pair at a time.\">F2L</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a>, <a href=\"/en/glossary#pll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Puts the last layer pieces in place, after OLL. There are 21 cases, named after letters (T, J, Y…); in 2-Look PLL, 6 cases in two parts.\">PLL</a>. It doesn&#39;t have a single inventor. The cross already appeared in 1978 (Taylor); F2L, <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a> by pair, was published by John Conway, David Seal and David Benson in 1979 and by several Dutch cubers in 1981. OLL and PLL (the last layer in two steps) came up at the same time in 1981: Hans Dockhorn and Anneke Treep in the Netherlands, and Jessica <a href=\"/en/glossary#fridrich\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Another name for CFOP, after Jessica Fridrich, who published the complete method in 1997.\">Fridrich</a> with Mirek Goljan in what was then Czechoslovakia; the letter names of the PLLs (T, J, Y…) come from him. Fridrich put it all together, completed the algorithms and published it on the internet in 1997; that&#39;s why the method became known as &quot;Fridrich&quot;, and it is the most used in the world.</p>\n",[138,139,140],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Methods/CFOP","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CFOP_method","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jessica_Fridrich","basico","The beginner method (layers)","<p>Solving in layers (<a href=\"/en/glossary#cruz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 4 edges of one face (usually white) in place, lined up with the centers. The first step of the beginner method and of CFOP.\">cross</a>, 1st layer corners, middle edges and the last layer in a few steps) is the oldest idea: the first known publication is by Donald Taylor, in 1978. David Singmaster, who started out solving the corners first, put together his layer version between 1979 and 1980 in the &quot;Notes on Rubik&#39;s Magic Cube&quot;, and James Nourse&#39;s book (1981), one of the best sellers of that year, took the method to the general public. It is almost everyone&#39;s path to this day.</p>\n",[145,146],"https://cubinghistory.com/3x3/Methods/LayerByLayer","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Layer-by-layer_method","3x3","The Rubik's Cube","<p>Hungarian Ernő Rubik, a professor of architecture and design in Budapest, created the cube in 1974 to show his students how pieces can turn without the whole thing falling apart, and it took him about a month to solve his own cube for the first time. Patented in Hungary as the &quot;Magic Cube&quot;, it reached the shops of Budapest in 1977; in 1980 Ideal Toy took the cube to the world under the name Rubik&#39;s Cube, and it became one of the best-selling toys in history. In 1982 Budapest hosted the first world championship, won by American Minh Thai with 22.95 seconds. The craze faded, but the internet brought the fast methods back in the late 1990s; in 2004 the World Cube Association (<a href=\"/en/glossary#wca\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The association that runs the official competitions and keeps the records.\">WCA</a>) was born, and it runs the official competitions to this day, where the fastest solve the 3x3 in under 5 seconds.</p>\n",[151,152,153],"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubik%27s_Cube","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Cube_Association","https://www.worldcubeassociation.org/about","/3x3/",{"_7":156,"_157":158},[161,162,163,164,165,166,167],"idiomas",[159,160],"pt","en",{"_21":147,"_32":147},{"_21":97,"_32":97},{"_21":66,"_32":66},{"_21":57,"_32":57},{"_21":47,"_32":48},{"_21":38,"_32":39},{"_21":22,"_32":24}]
